---
interface Props {
  pairs: any[];
}
const { pairs } = Astro.props;
const fmt = (value) => (value === null ? null : value.toFixed(4));
---

<table class="pairs">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th><button data-col="0">Pair</button></th>
      {/* Not sortable, deliberately. Behaviorally indistinguishable vectors can
          sit far apart in angle (arXiv:2602.06801), so ordering by this ranks on a
          quantity that does not mean what the ordering would imply. Every other
          column is the reader's to sort. */}
      <th>Angle similarity</th>
      <th>Checked by both</th>
      <th>Checked by only one</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    {pairs.map((p) => (
      <tr>
        <td class="mono">{p.a}<br />{p.b}</td>
        <td>
          {p.angle_similarity === null
            ? <span class="absent">not comparable</span>
            : fmt(p.angle_similarity)}
        </td>
        <td>
          {p.axes_both_checked.length
            ? p.axes_both_checked.join(", ")
            : <span class="absent">nothing</span>}
        </td>
        <td class="mono">
          {p.axes_only_a_checked.map((ax) => <div>{ax} (a only)</div>)}
          {p.axes_only_b_checked.map((ax) => <div>{ax} (b only)</div>)}
        </td>
      </tr>
    ))}
  </tbody>
</table>

<p class="note">
  Angle similarity is geometry and nothing else. Vectors that are indistinguishable
  in behavior can sit far apart in angle, so a low number here is not evidence that
  two authors reached different conclusions (arXiv:2602.06801). The rightmost column
  usually carries more: it is what one author thought to check and the other did not.
</p>

<script>
  // Pair rows arrive unordered and reorder only when a reader picks a column.
  document.querySelectorAll("table.pairs").forEach((table) => {
    const body = table.querySelector("tbody");
    table.querySelectorAll("th button").forEach((button) => {
      button.addEventListener("click", () => {
        const idx = Number(button.dataset.col);
        const rows = [...body.rows];
        rows.sort((x, y) => {
          const a = x.cells[idx].innerText.trim();
          const c = y.cells[idx].innerText.trim();
          const na = parseFloat(a);
          const nc = parseFloat(c);
          return !isNaN(na) && !isNaN(nc) ? nc - na : a.localeCompare(c);
        });
        rows.forEach((r) => body.appendChild(r));
      });
    });
  });
</script>
